          "derivation": "Persian بَم bam 'low, deep (of a voice or a string)', the opposite of زیر zir 'high', is genuinely Persian and not an Arabism: Borhān-e Qāṭeʿ p.303 defines it 'آوای خشن و درشت آدمی و ساز' and adds 'معرب آن هم «بمّ»' — that is, the Arabic بَمّ is the Arabicized form OF the Persian word, the reverse of an Arabic loan; Asbaghi p.56, the same page as balūṭ, lists Arabic bamm 'Baß' (pl. bumūm) among the Persian loanwords in Arabic. Mann (IEC) sets it under both bombo- and bhombhos (2) beside Greek bómbos and Sanskrit bhambhā 'kettledrum', and notes that Armenian bamb 'bass' is itself a loan from Persian. So Nourai's chart note is right that IEC calls it Indo-European and, as far as can be checked, wrong that Moʿin calls it Arabic: Farhang-e Moʿin s.v. بم gives '(بَ) (اِ.) صدای درشت و خشن آدمی و ساز. مق. زیر' with NO [ع.] tag. The reason for 'plausible' rather than 'confirmed' is the nature of the etymon: an echoic word of this shape can be created independently in any language, so 'descends from PIE *bamb-' cannot be demonstrated by sound law; بم may equally be a native Persian sound-word that merely resembles the family.",
